import { Link } from "react-router-dom";
import "./signup.css";
function Signup() {
    return (
        <div className="signup-container">
            <div className="signup-page">
                <div className="logo-section">
                    <h2>⏳ TimeCapsule</h2>
                </div>
                <div className="heading-section">
                    <h1>Create Account</h1>
                    <p className="signup-description">Start preserving your memories with TimeCapsule!</p>
                </div>
                <form className="signup-form">
                    <label>Full Name</label>
                    <input type="text" placeholder="Username" required />
                    <label>Email Address</label>
                    <input type="email" placeholder="Email" required />
                    <label>Password</label>
                    <input type="password" placeholder="Password" required />
                    <label>Confirm Password</label>
                    <input type="password" placeholder="Confirm Password" />
                    <button type="submit">Sign Up</button>
                </form>
                <p className="login-link">Already have an account? <Link to="/login">Login here</Link></p>
            </div>
        </div>
    )
}
export default Signup;

Excellent! 🚀

Karishma, you've already built:

✅ Landing Page

✅ Signup Page

Now let's build the Login Page, but this time I'm going to teach you a little differently.

In a company, a senior developer won't say:

"Copy the Signup page and remove two fields."

Instead, they'll ask:

"What is the purpose of the Login page?"

Let's answer that first.

🧠 Step 1: Think Before Coding
What is the purpose of the Signup page?
To create a new account.

That's why it needs:

Full Name

Email

Password

Confirm Password

What is the purpose of the Login page?
To verify an existing user.

So we only need:

Email

Password

That's it.

Notice how the business requirement decides what fields we need.

🏗 Step 2: Design the Login Page
Here's the layout we'll build.

+--------------------------------------+
|          ⏳ TimeCapsule              |
|                                      |
|          Welcome Back 👋             |
|                                      |
| Continue your journey by logging in. |
|                                      |
| Email Address                        |
| [__________________________]         |
|                                      |
| Password                             |
| [__________________________]         |
|                                      |
| Forgot Password?                     |
|                                      |
|        Login Button                  |
|                                      |
| Don't have an account? Sign Up       |
+--------------------------------------+
This should feel familiar to users because it matches the Signup page's design.

🎨 Step 3: Create Login.jsx
We'll use the same structure as Signup.

import { Link } from "react-router-dom";
import "./Login.css";

function Login() {
  return (
    <div className="login-container">

      <div className="login-card">

        {/* Logo */}
        <div className="logo-section">
          <h2>⏳ TimeCapsule</h2>
        </div>

        {/* Heading */}
        <div className="heading-section">
          <h1>Welcome Back 👋</h1>
          <p>Continue your journey by logging in.</p>
        </div>

        {/* Form */}
        <form className="login-form">

          <label>Email Address</label>
          <input
            type="email"
            placeholder="Enter your email"
            required
          />

          <label>Password</label>
          <input
            type="password"
            placeholder="Enter your password"
            required
          />

          <p className="forgot-password">
            Forgot Password?
          </p>

          <button type="submit">
            Login
          </button>

        </form>

        <p className="signup-link">
          Don't have an account?
          <Link to="/signup"> Sign Up</Link>
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  );
}

export default Login;
🎓 Today's React Concept: Reusability
Compare the two pages.

Signup
<div className="signup-container">
Login
<div className="login-container">
They are almost identical.

Professional developers immediately ask:

"Can I reuse this?"

Right now, we'll keep separate CSS files because it's easier to learn.

Later, I'll teach you how to create a reusable AuthLayout component that both Login and Signup can use. This reduces duplicate code and is a common professional practice.

🎨 Step 4: Login.css
Instead of writing everything from scratch, here's your task.

Create a new file:
Login.css
Copy the contents of Signup.css into it.

Then make these small changes:

Rename:

.signup-container → .login-container

.signup-page (or .signup-card if you renamed it) → .login-card

.signup-form → .login-form

.login-link → .signup-link

Everything else can stay the same for now.

🤔 Why not reuse the same CSS today?
Because we're still learning.

If I introduce reusable layouts and shared styles too early, you'll have to understand React components, props, CSS organization, and folder structure all at once.

Learning one concept at a time is much easier.

🎯 Mini Quiz
Why does the Login page not need a "Confirm Password" field?

Choose one:

A. Because the user already created and confirmed their password during signup.

B. Because React automatically checks passwords.

C. Because Login pages never use passwords.

Reply with A, B, or C.

🎯 Your Tasks
Create Login.css.

Copy the styles from Signup.css.

Rename the class names as mentioned above.

Create Login.jsx using the structure I shared.

Make sure the Login button and the Sign Up link are visible.

Once it's working, we'll:

Add a "Forgot Password?" interaction.

Connect the Login page to routing.

Then begin adding authentication logic (without a backend yet).

One last thing: you're making steady progress. A few weeks ago, you were asking how to start a React project. Now you're building multi-page applications with routing and responsive layouts. Keep building one feature at a time—that's exactly how real projects grow.


