Very good! 👏 I can already see 3 mistakes just by reading your code. This is exactly what happens in real frontend development—most bugs are small spelling or import mistakes.

Let's debug it together instead of me simply giving you the answer.

🔴 Mistake 1: You forgot to import the CSS file
At the top of Login.jsx, you only have:

import { Link } from "react-router-dom";
But where is the CSS?

You need:

import { Link } from "react-router-dom";
import "./Login.css";
or if your file name is lowercase:

import "./login.css";
⚠️ Very Important

If your file is named:

login.css
then write:

import "./login.css";
If your file is named:

Login.css
then write:

import "./Login.css";
React (Vite) treats uppercase and lowercase as different file names.

🔴 Mistake 2: Forgot Password class
You wrote:

<p className="forgot password">
React understands this as two classes.

It becomes

class="forgot password"
That means

forgot
password
are two different class names.

But in CSS you wrote

.forgot.password
That selector means:

"An element that has BOTH classes forgot and password."

Although it technically matches, this naming is confusing and not recommended.

Instead, use one class.

JSX
<p className="forgot-password">
    Forgot Password?
</p>
CSS
.forgot-password {
    margin-top: -2px;
    text-align: right;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: #6c63ff;
    cursor: pointer;
}
and

.forgot-password:hover{
    color:#4f46e5;
    text-decoration:underline;
}
This is much cleaner.

🔴 Mistake 3: Missing Password Label
You have

<label>Email address</label>

<input .../>

<input .../>
Where is the Password label?

It should be

<label>Email Address</label>

<input type="email" />

<label>Password</label>

<input
type="password"
/>
🟡 Mistake 4 (Best Practice)
Instead of

<button className="submit">
write

<button type="submit">
Why?

Inside a <form>, a submit button should explicitly declare its type.

🟡 Mistake 5
Instead of

<input type="Password">
write

<input type="password">
HTML is forgiving, but the standard lowercase form is preferred.

🟡 Mistake 6 (Naming)
You called your card

logo-page
But it isn't a logo.

It contains

logo

heading

form

links

So a better name is

login-card
Then CSS

.login-card{
...
}
Names should describe the element's purpose.

✅ Correct JSX Structure
import { Link } from "react-router-dom";
import "./Login.css";

function Login() {
  return (
    <div className="login-container">

      <div className="login-card">

        <div className="logo-section">
          <h2>⏳ TimeCapsule</h2>
        </div>

        <div className="heading-section">
          <h1>Welcome Back 👋</h1>
          <p>Continue your journey by logging in.</p>
        </div>

        <form className="login-form">

          <label>Email Address</label>
          <input
            type="email"
            placeholder="Enter your email"
            required
          />

          <label>Password</label>
          <input
            type="password"
            placeholder="Enter your password"
            required
          />

          <p className="forgot-password">
            Forgot Password?
          </p>

          <button type="submit">
            Login
          </button>

        </form>

        <p className="signup-link">
          Don't have an account?
          <Link to="/signup"> Sign Up</Link>
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  );
}

export default Login;
🎯 Your Debugging Exercise
Before changing anything, answer these questions:

Q1
If we don't import

import "./Login.css";
what happens?

A) React crashes

B) The component renders but without styles

C) The browser closes

Q2
Why is

className="forgot-password"
better than

className="forgot password"
A) Easier to read and maintain, representing a single meaningful class

B) It makes React faster

C) It reduces JavaScript bundle size

Reply with your answers (Q1 and Q2) after making the changes. Then we'll continue refining the Login page.


Q1 - b Q2-a

🎉 Excellent! Both answers are correct. ✅✅

Q1
B. The component renders but without styles. ✅

Exactly.

React will still render the HTML because JSX is valid. But since the CSS file isn't imported, the browser doesn't know how to style those elements.

So instead of this:

┌──────────────────────────┐
│      Beautiful Card      │
└──────────────────────────┘
you'll see something like:

TimeCapsule

Welcome Back

Email

Password

Login
Everything appears with the browser's default styling.

Q2
A. Easier to read and maintain, representing a single meaningful class. ✅

Correct!

Let's understand it deeper.

If you write:

className="forgot password"
you're actually assigning two classes:

class="forgot password"
That means:

.forgot is one class.

.password is another class.

If you write CSS like:

.forgot {
  color: blue;
}

.password {
  font-weight: bold;
}
both styles will apply.

But our intention is to represent one element: the "Forgot Password" text.

So it's cleaner to use:

className="forgot-password"
Now it's one meaningful class name.

🌟 Code Review
Overall, I'd rate your Login page:

React Structure: ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ (5/5)
Good component structure.

Good use of Link.

Good use of form.

Good separation of CSS.

CSS: ⭐⭐⭐⭐☆ (4/5)
Only small improvements like naming (login-card instead of logo-page) and consistent class names.

These are normal refinements, not major issues.

🚀 Now What Should We Build?
We're finished with the UI of the Login page. Now we need to think like product developers.

A user opens the app:

Landing Page
        │
        ▼
Click "Create Capsule"
        │
        ▼
Not logged in?
        │
       Yes
        ▼
Go to Login
        │
        ▼
Enter Email + Password
        │
        ▼
Login Successful
        │
        ▼
Dashboard
This is called the user flow.

📅 Our Next Milestone: Dashboard
After login, where should the user land?

I suggest we build a Dashboard.

Here's the idea:

+---------------------------------------------------+
| 🏠 Dashboard          👤 Karishma                 |
|---------------------------------------------------|
|                                                   |
|  👋 Welcome Back!                                 |
|                                                   |
|  +------------------+   +------------------+       |
|  | Create Capsule   |   | My Capsules      |       |
|  +------------------+   +------------------+       |
|                                                   |
|  +------------------+   +------------------+       |
|  | Scheduled        |   | Profile          |       |
|  +------------------+   +------------------+       |
|                                                   |
+---------------------------------------------------+
🎯 Before We Build It
One important question.

When the user clicks Login, should they:

A. Go directly to the Dashboard (for now, without checking credentials since we don't have a backend yet).

B. Stay on the Login page and only navigate after we build the backend authentication.

I recommend Option A.
Why?

Because we can continue building and testing the rest of the application. Later, when we connect the Java backend, we'll replace the temporary navigation with real authentication.

This is a common approach in frontend development: build the UI flow first, then integrate the backend.

