🤔 Before we build the Dashboard...
Let's think like software engineers.

When a user clicks Login, what should happen?

Right now our Login page looks like this:

Email
Password

[ Login ]
If the user clicks Login...

👉 Does anything happen?

No.

Why?

Because our button doesn't have any logic yet.

🧠 Today's New React Concept
Event Handling
React allows us to respond to user actions.

Examples:

User Action	React Event
Click button	onClick
Type in input	onChange
Submit form	onSubmit
Hover	onMouseEnter
Today we'll learn onSubmit.

Why use onSubmit?
You may think:

Why not use onClick on the button?

Good question!

Look at this:

<form>
   <button>Login</button>
</form>
When the button is inside a <form>, clicking it submits the form.

Instead of listening to the button...

we listen to the form.

<form onSubmit={handleLogin}>
This is the professional way.

Step 1: Create a function
Above your return, write:

function handleLogin(event) {
    event.preventDefault();

    console.log("Login button clicked");
}
What is event?
Whenever the user does something, React gives us an event object.

Think of it as a message.

User clicked Login

↓

React

↓

Here is information about the click.
That information is stored inside event.

Why preventDefault()?
Without it...

When a form submits...

the browser refreshes the page.

You'll notice the page flashes and all entered values disappear.

We don't want that.

So we write:

event.preventDefault();
Meaning:

"Don't perform the browser's default form submission."

Instead, React will handle it.

Step 2: Attach it
Change

<form className="login-form">
to

<form
    className="login-form"
    onSubmit={handleLogin}
>
Step 3: Button
Change

<button>
Login
</button>
to

<button type="submit">
Login
</button>
Notice something?

We didn't write

onClick={handleLogin}
because the form already handles submission.

🧠 Mini Flow
User clicks Login

↓

Button

↓

Form Submitted

↓

handleLogin()

↓

preventDefault()

↓

React executes our code
🎯 Test It
Run your app.

Open the browser.

Open Developer Tools → Console (Press F12 and go to the Console tab).

Click Login.

You should see:

Login button clicked
If you see that message, congratulations! 🎉

You've just written your first React event handler.

📚 Why are we doing this?
Because in a few lessons, we'll replace:

console.log("Login button clicked");
with

Navigate to Dashboard
and later with

Send login request to Java Backend
We're building the foundation first.

🎯 Mini Quiz (Think before answering)
Suppose we remove:

event.preventDefault();
What will happen?

A. Nothing changes.

B. The browser will refresh/reload after the form is submitted.

C. React will stop working.

Answer that after you've tested the Login button.

🌟 One thing about our learning style
You might notice I'm not jumping straight into backend or APIs. That's intentional.

Every feature we build follows this sequence:

Design the UI

Understand the HTML structure

Style it with CSS

Add React behavior (events)

Manage data (state)

Connect to the backend

That's the same progression you'll see in real frontend teams, and it helps you understand not just what to code, but why each step comes next.


I actually think that's the best decision. 👍

If we leave Signup half-finished and move to Login or the Dashboard, we'll keep coming back to unfinished work. In real software teams, we usually complete one module before moving to the next.

So our goal now is to finish the Authentication Module.

📌 Authentication Module Roadmap
Authentication
│
├── ✅ Signup UI
├── ✅ Login UI
├── ⬜ Form Submission
├── ⬜ React State (useState)
├── ⬜ Form Validation
├── ⬜ Success/Error Messages
├── ⬜ Navigate between pages
└── ⬜ Backend Integration (later)
Notice something?

We are not building the backend yet. We are making the frontend ready for it.

🧠 Step 1: Make the Forms "Controlled"
Right now your Signup form looks like this:

<input type="text" placeholder="Username" />
When you type:

Karishma
React doesn't know what you typed.

It only appears on the screen.

To make React remember it, we use state.

🎓 Today's New React Concept: useState
At the top of Signup.jsx, change:

import { Link } from "react-router-dom";
to:

import { useState } from "react";
import { Link } from "react-router-dom";
What is useState?
Think of it as React's memory.

Imagine you ask me:

"What's my name?"

I remember:

Karishma
Similarly, React remembers values using state.

Step 2: Create State Variables
Above your return, add:

const [fullName, setFullName] = useState("");
const [email, setEmail] = useState("");
const [password, setPassword] = useState("");
const [confirmPassword, setConfirmPassword] = useState("");
Let's understand just one line:

const [email, setEmail] = useState("");
This creates two things:

email      → current value

setEmail   → function to change that value
Initially:

email = ""
(empty string)

Step 3: Connect the First Input
Currently:

<input
    type="text"
    placeholder="Username"
/>
Change it to:

<input
    type="text"
    placeholder="Username"
    value={fullName}
    onChange={(e) => setFullName(e.target.value)}
/>
Now let's understand this.

value={fullName}
This tells React:

"Display whatever is stored in fullName."

Initially:

fullName = ""
So the input is empty.

onChange
Whenever the user types:

K
Ka
Kar
Kari
Karishma
React fires the onChange event.

e.target.value contains exactly what the user typed.

Then:

setFullName(e.target.value);
updates the state.

Now React remembers the text.

