For now, don't change all four inputs.

I want you to do only the Full Name field.

After that, add this just above the return:

console.log(fullName);
Now:

Run your app.

Open the browser console (F12 → Console).

Type into the Full Name field.

You should see:

K
Ka
Kar
Kari
Karishma
printed in the console as you type.

🤔 Why are we going one input at a time?
Because I don't want you to memorize.

I want you to understand.

Once you understand one input:

Email ✅

Password ✅

Confirm Password ✅

will all follow the same pattern.

🧠 Homework Question
Without looking at my answer, think about this:

If we write:

const [email, setEmail] = useState("");
Why do we need two variables?

Why not just:

const email = useState("");
We'll discuss this after you've completed the Full Name field. Understanding that answer will help you grasp one of the core ideas behind React state.


it is done next

Excellent! 🎉

You just completed one of the most important React concepts. Don't underestimate it—almost every React application uses useState().

Now we'll finish the Signup form by applying the same pattern to the remaining fields.

Step 4: Add State to All Inputs
You already have:

const [fullName, setFullName] = useState("");
const [email, setEmail] = useState("");
const [password, setPassword] = useState("");
const [confirmPassword, setConfirmPassword] = useState("");
Now update the remaining inputs.

Email
<input
    type="email"
    placeholder="Enter your email"
    value={email}
    onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)}
    required
/>
Password
<input
    type="password"
    placeholder="Enter your password"
    value={password}
    onChange={(e) => setPassword(e.target.value)}
    required
/>
Confirm Password
<input
    type="password"
    placeholder="Confirm your password"
    value={confirmPassword}
    onChange={(e) => setConfirmPassword(e.target.value)}
    required
/>
🧠 What happens now?
Suppose you type:

Name     : Karishma
Email    : karishma@gmail.com
Password : abc123
React stores them like this:

fullName = "Karishma"

email = "karishma@gmail.com"

password = "abc123"

confirmPassword = "abc123"
Now React knows what the user typed.

Earlier, only the browser knew.

This is why these are called Controlled Components.

Step 5: Create handleSignup
Above return, write:

function handleSignup(event) {
    event.preventDefault();

    console.log("Full Name:", fullName);
    console.log("Email:", email);
    console.log("Password:", password);
    console.log("Confirm Password:", confirmPassword);
}
Step 6: Connect the Form
Change:

<form className="signup-form">
to

<form
    className="signup-form"
    onSubmit={handleSignup}
>
Step 7: Test It
Fill the form:

Full Name: Karishma

Email: karishma@gmail.com

Password: abc123

Confirm Password: abc123
Click Sign Up.

Open the Console (F12).

You should see:

Full Name: Karishma

Email: karishma@gmail.com

Password: abc123

Confirm Password: abc123
🎉 Congratulations! You've just built your first controlled React form.

🎓 Why are we doing this?
Imagine later we connect the backend.

Instead of:

console.log(email);
we'll send the data to the server:

{
  fullName: "Karishma",
  email: "karishma@gmail.com",
  password: "abc123"
}
So useState is the bridge between the user interface and the backend.

🚀 After This Comes Validation
Once you've confirmed the form logs the data correctly, we'll make it smarter.

We'll check things like:

❌ Is the Full Name empty?

❌ Is the Email valid?

❌ Is the Password at least 8 characters?

❌ Do Password and Confirm Password match?

If something is wrong, we'll show a friendly error message without refreshing the page.

This is how professional web applications work.

🎯 Mini Quiz
If the user types:

Karishma
into the Full Name field, which function updates the state?

A.

setFullName(e.target.value)
B.

fullName(e.target.value)
C.

useState(e.target.value)
Answer after you've tested your Signup form. If everything works, we'll move on to form validation, which is the next essential skill for building production-ready React applications.


a

🎉 Correct! ✅

setFullName(e.target.value)
is the function that updates the value of fullName.

Think of it like this:

User types "Karishma"

        ↓

onChange event fires

        ↓

e.target.value = "Karishma"

        ↓

setFullName("Karishma")

        ↓

React updates

fullName = "Karishma"

        ↓

UI re-renders automatically
This automatic re-rendering is one of React's biggest strengths.

🚀 Now let's make the Signup page smarter (Validation)
Right now, if a user enters:

Name: Karishma
Email: karishma@gmail.com
Password: 12345678
Confirm Password: 87654321
and clicks Sign Up, our console still prints the values.

But should we allow the user to create an account?

No! The passwords don't match.

A professional application validates the data before sending it to the backend.

Step 1: Add Password Validation
Open your handleSignup() function.

It probably looks like this:

function handleSignup(event) {
    event.preventDefault();

    console.log(fullName);
    console.log(email);
    console.log(password);
    console.log(confirmPassword);
}
Now change it to:

function handleSignup(event) {
    event.preventDefault();

    if (password !== confirmPassword) {
        alert("Passwords do not match!");
        return;
    }

    console.log("Signup Successful!");
    console.log(fullName);
    console.log(email);
}
Let's understand it line by line
if
if (password !== confirmPassword)
means:

"If the password is not equal to the confirm password..."

For example:

Password        = abc12345
ConfirmPassword = xyz12345
They are different.

So the condition is true.

alert()
alert("Passwords do not match!");
shows a popup:

-------------------------
| Passwords do not match |
|            OK          |
-------------------------
return
This line is very important.

return;
means:

"Stop executing the rest of this function."

Without it, React would continue and print:

Signup Successful!
even though the passwords were wrong.

🧪 Test It
Test Case 1
Password: abc12345

Confirm Password: xyz12345
Expected:

✅ Alert appears

Passwords do not match!
Test Case 2
Password: abc12345

Confirm Password: abc12345
Expected:

Console:

Signup Successful!

Karishma

karishma@gmail.com
🧠 New Concept: Validation Flow
User clicks Sign Up

        │
        ▼

Passwords Match?

      /     \
    No       Yes
    │         │
    ▼         ▼
 Show Alert  Continue
              │
              ▼
      Send data to backend (later)
Every real application follows a similar flow before accepting user input.

📚 Why are we using alert()?
Only because we're learning.

In a real application, a popup isn't the best user experience.

Later, we'll display the error below the input field, like this:

Password
[********]

Confirm Password
[********]

❌ Passwords do not match
That looks much more professional, and we'll build it ourselves.

